export const enCopy = {
"ressources/mobilier-terrasse-piscine-hotel": ["Mobilier de terrasse et de piscine pour hôtels : matériaux et fixations", "Mobilier de terrasse et pool deck", "Teak, black locust, powder-coated aluminium or woven resin: how to choose hotel terrace and pool furniture, fixings per substrate and glides that match the deck.", "A lounger that creaks in its third season, a table rusting at the base, a parasol tipping over at the first gust: on a pool deck these incidents are not settled by a claim, they are settled by early replacement. Hotel terrace furniture is therefore handled as a technical package, with four decisions locked before the tender: the material family, the fixing type, the connection to the ground and the coherence across terrace, pool deck and garden. What follows does not rank materials by prestige: it ranks them by what they actually withstand over some ten years of operation, salt, chlorine, UV and daily handling included.", [["Which materials best resist salt, chlorine and UV on a hotel terrace?", "No material resists everything: teak and black locust hold up to salt water but suffer from neglected maintenance, aluminium tolerates chlorine and washing but concentrates the risk in its fixings, and woven resin resists UV only if its HDPE is stabilised and its frame remains aluminium. The choice between these four material families is therefore made by elimination according to real exposure, not by a hierarchy of prestige. Teak (Tectona grandis) owes its reputation to two wood compounds: tectoquinone, which limits insect attack, and a silica content that seals the surface against water while wearing out tools. On a hotel terrace this translates into a service life generally between ten and fifteen years without fungicide, provided the timber comes from a managed plantation and is properly dried before machining. Teak dried too fast moves, opens its joints and turns white in patches from the first season. The moisture content of the batch is checked before machining, generally 8 to 12% depending on origin, and batches above the upper limit are refused, even when the price per cubic metre is attractive. A chain-of-custody certification such as FSC does not cover this point: it documents origin, not drying. Black locust (Robinia pseudoacacia), sold as false acacia, grows in Central Europe. Its hardness exceeds teak and its natural durability places it in use class 4 without treatment, allowing direct ground contact. It greys faster and takes on a silvery tone close to aged teak, with no oiling. Two operating reservations matter: weight, which complicates end-of-season handling, and checking, frequent on insufficiently stabilised solid sections. Aluminium 6060 or 6063, T6 treated then powder-coated, is the easiest material to operate on a pool deck: it does not rust, does not swell, accepts hosing and diluted chlorine, and stacks without splitting. Two points are checked at order: coating thickness, generally 60 to 80 µm for class 2, and the treatment of cut edges, because a bare cut becomes the entry point for filiform corrosion, especially in the coastal band. Surface temperature rises fast in the sun; on seating, weaving or textile remains necessary. Woven resin means HDPE yarns woven around a core, and quality depends not on the pattern but on three parameters: UV stabilisation of the HDPE, an aluminium core, and solution-dyed yarn. In a tender, the only useful question remains: can the supplier produce the test report of the delivered batch, not the one from the trade-show collection? In summary: teak — very good by the sea if drying is controlled, optional oiling and gentle cleaning, 10 to 15 years depending on exposure, main limitation price and batch variability; black locust — very good, use class 4, no oiling, 12 to 18 years observed, main limitations weight and checking; powder-coated aluminium — good, cut edges to protect, washing with water and neutral soap, 10 to 15 years depending on the finish, main limitation corrosion at the fixings; woven resin — good if HDPE is stabilised, hose cleaning without solvent, 5 to 9 years on the most exposed pieces, main limitation weaving replacement."], ["Teak, black locust, aluminium or woven resin: what is the ten-year operating cost?", "Over ten years, the purchase price is only part of the spend: teak costs more upfront and little to maintain if the silvered look is accepted, aluminium costs less and needs almost nothing, woven resin costs the least but is replaced earlier on the most exposed pieces. The right indicator is therefore not the unit price but the full operating cost over the seasons actually served. Three cost items weigh heavily and disappear from price comparisons. The first is storage labour: a 12–18 kg solid-wood lounger takes two people, a 6–9 kg aluminium lounger takes one, and this difference becomes staff hours every evening of high season. The second is parasol mast breakage, often costlier than seating breakage, because a fallen parasol takes the neighbouring table with it. The third is replacement logistics: a part available in 45 to 60 days gets replaced, a part available in six months condemns the area. On a project in Doha (Qatar), a batch of woven-resin tables bought for their price lasted three seasons on the pool deck, then were replaced by aluminium tops and better-quality woven seating: the initial saving had been absorbed by two replacement campaigns and the time spent handling complaints. The same pattern returns in Muscat (Oman) on pieces left outside all year without shade. There is nonetheless a cost argument that holds: at equal specification, direct factory supply often sits 30 to 50% below a European brand quotation, provided documented quality control and a 45 to 60 day production lead time are accepted. The comparison only makes sense if the specification is identical on both sides: same profile thickness, same coating class, same assembly quality and same timber moisture control. On an indicative ten-year model, acquisition accounts for roughly 55–65% of total cost in teak, 50–60% in black locust, 60–70% in aluminium and 45–55% in woven resin; maintenance and cleaning 15–20% in teak and resin, 10–15% in black locust and aluminium; part replacement 10–15% for the first three families and 20–30% for resin; storage labour 5–10% in teak and aluminium, 10–15% in black locust and resin. This model is recalculated with the project's volumes and hourly rates, since the ranges vary with exposure and season length. The tiering logic described for hotel guestroom furniture applies outdoors too, with one extra criterion: the most exposed piece is not the one receiving the most guests, it is the one that stays in sun and spray all year."], ["Which fixings to choose per substrate: concrete slab, joist, gravel or lawn?", "Fixings are chosen per substrate, not per habit: a concrete slab accepts a mechanical or chemical anchor in A4 stainless steel, a composite joist requires a countersunk A4 stainless screw with pre-drilling, gravel calls for a ballasted base without anchoring, and lawn a stake or a load-spreading plate. Each substrate has its own load-path logic, and these fixings rarely transfer from one substrate to another. On concrete, the critical point is not the anchor but the edge distance: an anchor set too close to a slab edge cracks the concrete before it holds, and the crack becomes a water path. A dimensioned anchoring plan is requested, with rebar positions where drawings exist, and a four-point base plate is preferred over a central foot on wind-exposed terrace tables. Right next to the pool, chemical anchoring remains possible but becomes delicate: resin dislikes a damp substrate and chlorine attacks some formulations; a ballasted, removable base plate avoids the problem. On composite or timber joists, countersunk A4 stainless screws with pre-drilling are the baseline; composite moves with temperature and a screw set without pre-drilling opens a crack that lengthens over one summer. Hidden clip fixings work on decking boards, not on load-bearing structures. On a timber deck, the washer matters as much as the screw: a washer too small marks the wood, a non-isolated stainless washer creates a galvanic couple with the aluminium of the base. On gravel and sand, no point anchoring is reliable: the solution is a heavy base, precast concrete or a fillable tray, whose mass is calculated from the wind load of the piece. On lawn, aim for a through stake or a buried spreading plate; with automatic irrigation the stake rises and the piece tips. Finally, on pool coping, drilling is almost always a bad idea: coping is often glued and drilling opens a water path under the tiling. A ballasted base set on the coping, with a soft glide, solves the need without touching the waterproofing. Summarised per substrate: concrete slab — 4-point base plate and A4 stainless anchor, avoid an anchor near the edge, check edge distance with a dimensioned plan; composite joist — A4 stainless screw with pre-drilling, avoid zinc-plated wood screws, check for crack initiation; gravel and sand — ballasted base or fillable tray, avoid a stake without a mass, check mass and wind load; lawn — through stake or spreading plate, avoid a simply set foot, check hold after watering; pool coping — ballasted base and soft glide, avoid drilling the coping, check waterproofing and level."], ["How to choose feet and glides for wind, standing water and cleaning?", "Feet transfer three things to the ground: weight, lateral wind load, and the water running down the leg; choosing glides therefore means trading off slip, grip and drainage, with levelling adjusters compensating the slopes of a pool deck. A glide chosen to protect tiling can, at the same time, make the furniture unstable on a wet deck. The first trap is the felt glide, common on indoor pieces and disastrous outside: it absorbs water, swells, detaches and leaves a ring on stone. The second is the black filled-rubber glide, which migrates on light decks and leaves marks that are hard to remove. Drainage matters as much as material: an open-bottom tubular leg draws in water by capillarity, keeps it in the profile and releases it as a rust stain at the weld. Legs are delivered with closed end caps and drainage holes drilled at the lowest point, and this detail is verified on the assembly sheet rather than on the photo. On a project in Nice (France), a batch of tables delivered with simply pushed-in end caps saw, after one winter, trapped water burst the powder coating at the base; replacing the end caps cost less than reworking the frames. Wind is handled at the base, not the canopy: a 3 m parasol on a base of about twenty kilos lies down at the first storm, whatever the fabric quality. Water-fillable bases are practical in summer and awkward in winter, when freezing water splits the tray; sand weighs more per volume but travels badly. For stackable seating, a strap or shrink film over the stack prevents the sail effect. Finally, removable covers with quick-drain foam dry in about an hour, solid-foam cushions stay damp for two days or more and smell of chlorine. For choice by surface: PTFE or light polyamide glide on slab, stone and tiling — slides without marking but grips poorly on wet ground; studded EPDM glide on composite and timber — good grip but may mark light floors; filled polypropylene glide on concrete and compacted gravel — resistant and economical but crushes under heavy load; ballasted base on gravel and coping — stable and removable but bulky and heavy in logistics."], ["Which specifications and tests to require in an outdoor furniture tender?", "An outdoor tender is judged on five documents: the reports of EN 581-1, EN 581-2 and EN 581-3 tests, the salt-spray and coating-adhesion test records, the assembly sheet with the anchoring plan, the spare-parts list and the approved sample. Without these five documents, the compared price compares nothing, and the best offer on paper becomes the costliest in use. The EN 581 series covers outdoor furniture for domestic, communal and camping use: part 1 covers general safety requirements, part 2 seats, part 3 tables. The corresponding test methods, notably EN 1728 for seating and EN 1022 for stability, say how loads are applied, how tipping is tested and how measurements are taken. A serious supplier cites them with the laboratory and the report date; a supplier who answers 'compliant with European standards' without a report number has no report. Powder coating is documented by two tests: salt spray per ISO 9227, with a duration depending on the target class, and adhesion per ISO 2409, with an expected class of 1 or better. For woven pieces, request the UV ageing report per ISO 4892-3 and the HDPE composition. For timber, request the species, origin, moisture content before machining and, where applicable, the chain-of-custody certificate. Three clauses deserve to be written black on white in the contract: spare-parts availability duration, expressed in years and not 'subject to stock'; the warranty structure, split between frame, finish and weaving, because a single five-year warranty often hides a finish exclusion; the sample approval procedure, with a validation lead time and a clear rule on colour variation between prototype and series. Manufacturing control is also verified: a supplier audit per ISO 9001 documents a system, not a batch; ask in addition for the batch control plan, coating-thickness records and treatment-bath traceability. The thresholds to request, item by item: general safety EN 581-1, dated report with identified laboratory; outdoor seating EN 581-2, compliant load and stability; outdoor tables EN 581-3, stability test included; salt spray ISO 9227, duration per class and report of the delivered batch; coating adhesion ISO 2409, class 1 or better; UV ageing ISO 4892-3, report on the delivered HDPE."], ["How to build a coherent package across terrace, pool deck and garden?", "A coherent terrace and pool furniture package is built by zone: the covered terrace favours comfort and the warmth of timber, the pool deck favours aluminium and resin for lightness and fast drying, the garden favours robustness and ground anchoring. Coherence does not come from a single material applied everywhere; it comes from a palette, a vocabulary of shapes and a finish level held from one zone to the next. Piece counts follow operations, not the architect's plan: the ratio sits rather between 0.6 and 1.2 loungers per room depending on the number of pools and the clientele, and a restaurant terrace needs a cover count equal to seated capacity plus a 10–15% reserve for breakage and replacement. These ratios are checked with the operator before the quantities are frozen, because they also determine the size of the storage area. Storage is the poor relation of outdoor packages: a stack of ten loungers occupies about 1.5–2.5 m² of floor, provided the geometry allows it; a badly designed folding table takes more room than a fixed one. In an urban hotel, storage is often an unventilated technical room where cushions stay damp; breathable covers and low-level ventilation cost less than replacing the foam after two years. The finish palette is locked early: two different suppliers on the same pool deck produce two shades of grey because of the powder batch, and the difference shows in full sun. If the package is phased, either order all the powder at once or accept a written colour tolerance. The hotel furniture checklist helps not to forget the accessory pieces — ashtrays, beach trolleys, poolside racks — which carry little value and many complaints. Finally, the outdoor package must plan its own renewal: a pool-deck chair is often replaced by two or three units each season, not thirty. Keeping the reference, shade and technical sheet of the batch allows unit orders three years later. By zone: covered terrace — armchairs and low tables, comfort and warm touch, teak or black locust; pool deck — loungers, tables and parasols, drying, weight and washing, aluminium and resin; garden — benches and dining tables, anchoring and robustness, black locust or aluminium; private beach — low loungers and trolleys, sand, wind and storage, aluminium or light resin. Hongye Furniture Group has supported hotel projects since 1996 with outdoor ranges produced under the same control requirements as indoor furniture; for a terrace and pool package, the FF&E team works from plans, quantities and anchoring constraints, then provides assembly sheets, test reports and samples before launch, with sample confirmation following the documented procedure that locks shade, material and finish before the series."]], ["Material family per exposure zone: salt, chlorine, UV and storage", "Fixing type per substrate: slab, joist, gravel, lawn, pool coping", "Glides and leg drainage: material, colour and closed end caps", "Test reports EN 581 / ISO 9227 / ISO 2409 / ISO 4892-3 for the delivered batch", "Assembly sheet with dimensioned anchoring plan and spare-parts list", "Finish palette locked and written colour tolerance if the package is phased"], [{"q": "Which timber should be chosen for a hotel lounger by the sea?", "a": "Teak remains the safest choice for seating in direct contact with saline air, provided a properly dried batch with traceable origin is required. Black locust offers higher hardness and needs no oiling, but its weight complicates daily storage. On a lounger handled twice a day, this criterion often weighs more than service life."}, {"q": "Does aluminium get too hot in the sun on a pool deck?", "a": "An unprotected aluminium profile reaches a high surface temperature in full sun, which makes seating uncomfortable. The solution is not to change material but to cover the contact zones: weaving, technical textile or a composite top on the armrests. Frames and legs stay in aluminium, where heat causes no usability problem."}, {"q": "Should pool coping be drilled to fix a parasol?", "a": "In most cases, no. Coping is often glued onto a shell and drilling opens a water path under the tiling that is hard to repair. A ballasted base set on the coping, with a soft glide and a mass calculated from the parasol's wind load, covers the need without touching the waterproofing. Drilling is only justified on a documented structural slab."}, {"q": "Which fasteners should be used on an aluminium frame by the sea?", "a": "A4 stainless steel, formerly 316, with isolation between the screw and the aluminium. A2 stainless is enough in urban atmospheres but holds up less well against chlorides. The isolating washer or nylon bushing prevents the galvanic couple that perforates the profile around the hole. A zinc-plated screw, however treated, remains a stopgap, not a project specification."}, {"q": "How long does hotel terrace furniture last?", "a": "Service life depends more on exposure and maintenance than on the material alone. Five to fifteen years is generally observed for a properly specified package, with gaps linked to material and exposure: maintained teak lasts longer, woven resin in full southern sun is replaced earlier. The pieces closest to the pool are always affected first."}, {"q": "Can the furniture be left outside all year?", "a": "Yes for the structure, with reservations. Powder-coated aluminium and black locust cope with winter, provided water does not stand in the legs and the glides are suitable. Cushions, straps and weavings degrade faster if left exposed. On a windy site, light pieces are strapped or stored, even in summer."}, {"q": "What lead time should be planned for a complete outdoor package?", "a": "Count generally 45 to 60 days of production after validation of samples and anchoring plans, to which transport and customs clearance are added. Sample validation itself takes one to two weeks depending on the number of finishes. On a phased project, ordering spare parts with the first delivery avoids a shortage in high season."}]],
"ressources/cahier-des-charges-ffe-appel-offres-hotelier": ["FF&E specifications: structuring a hotel tender", "Structuring an FF&E tender", "How to split a hotel FF&E brief into packages, set quantities by room type, fix finishes, write the mock-up clause, an evaluation grid and the exclusions to state before sending to suppliers.", "An FF&E tender is decided before it is sent: in how the brief splits packages, sets quantities by room type and describes finishes. When those three points stay vague, replies arrive on different scopes and comparing prices stops meaning anything. This article sets out a brief structure that lets you compare, negotiate and receive without starting the analysis again.", [["Why does the package split decide how offers can be compared?", "A hotel project mixes furniture families with different tolerances, lead times and installation constraints: guestrooms, bathrooms, reception, restaurant, meeting rooms and corridors. Sent as one package, the tender forces every manufacturer to price items it does not produce, so the returned figure becomes an assembly price that is hard to audit. Split into packages, it can be compared line by line and the gap becomes visible immediately.", "A workable split follows three rules: one package per technical family (guestroom furniture, upholstered seating, restaurant furniture, joinery), one package per zone where finishes differ strongly, and a separate package for installation and logistics. Each package carries its own schedule, finishes and exclusions, so a reply cannot hide behind a neighbouring item. The split also makes the mock-up clause negotiable, because a sample room can be signed off package by package on different dates."], ["How do you set quantities by room type without getting it wrong?", "Quantities are read by room type, never as one global total. A 120-key hotel can hold four or five types with different layouts: standard, accessible, superior and suite. The schedule must list, for each type, the composition item by item, then multiply by the number of keys. That matrix, not a total, is what later drives the delivery check.", "Two columns prevent most disputes: contracted quantity and reserve quantity. The reserve covers site breakage, sample rooms and replacement during the first months of operation. It must be stated in the brief, otherwise each manufacturer interprets it differently and may or may not include it in the price. Quantities must also be tied to a dated drawing revision; a schedule without a drawing reference becomes unusable at the first architect revision."], ["What belongs in the finishes schedule?", "The finishes schedule is the document that links the drawing to the price. For each element it lists the surface reference, colour, grain direction, edge type, hardware and expected resistance level. Without it, two manufacturers price two different qualities under the same label of pale oak, and the price gap measures only the material gap.", "Best practice is one visual reference per finish, with a version number and a date, and variants priced separately as options so they can be arbitrated at the end of negotiation without reopening the whole schedule. Technical finishes such as fire performance of upholstery, cleanability of surfaces and edge protection need their own section: they are compliance requirements, not colours, and should be presented as such."], ["How should the mock-up room clause be written?", "The mock-up room turns a brief into an enforceable reference. The clause must state the exact content, item by item, with the selected finishes; the lead time after shop drawings are approved; and the validation process, meaning who inspects, who signs, within what period, and what happens if a reservation is raised.", "One point is often missed: a mock-up room is only a reference if it is photographed and documented at signature. The minutes should include photographs, finish references and any tolerances accepted. That document, not the memory of the participants, serves for checking the following rooms. The clause should also cover reversibility: whether the room returns to operation, is dismantled, or its items are reused, since each option has a cost that belongs in the offer."], ["On what criteria should replies be evaluated?", "An evaluation grid written before offers are opened keeps the decision from resting on price alone. The useful criteria are few but must be weighted: compliance with the schedule, quality and consistency of proposed finishes, ability to hold the programme, comparable project references, warranty and after-sales terms, and then price.", "Two criteria decide hotel projects. The first is clarity of exclusions: an offer that does not state what it leaves out will be completed by variations. The second is the quality control method announced: inspection plan, acceptance criteria and how non-conformities are handled. A manufacturer who describes the method is easier to manage than one who promises an outcome. The grid should also provide a provisional ranking and a question phase, because a poorly written reply can hide a solid offer."], ["Which exclusions must be stated in writing?", "The exclusion list protects both sides. It must state what is not included: unloading and handling on site, storage, assembly, electrical connections, floor and paint repairs after installation, removal of packaging, and training of floor staff. Each of these has a real cost and will eventually be claimed by someone.", "The brief must also list interfaces: who supplies the lighting, who drills walls for sockets, who adjusts cabinet doors after the finished floor is laid. Unassigned interfaces are the first cause of delay on hotel sites, well ahead of manufacturing lead times. An interface table with an owner column and a reference document column is usually enough: it is short, it reads in five minutes, and it removes entire site meetings."]], ["One package per technical family and finish zone, each with its own schedule", "Quantities by room type, with contracted and reserve quantities", "A dated drawing reference for every schedule", "A versioned finishes schedule with one visual reference per finish", "Mock-up clause: content, lead time, validation, reservations, reversibility", "A weighted evaluation grid written before offers are opened", "An exclusions and interfaces table with owner and reference document"], [{"q": "When is the right time to send an FF&E brief?", "a": "As soon as room type drawings are fixed and finishes are referenced, even if some decisions remain open. Waiting for the final version costs weeks; it is better to send with separately priced options."}, {"q": "One package or several for a 120-key hotel?", "a": "Several, at minimum by technical family. A single package prevents line-by-line comparison and pushes scope differences between manufacturers into the final price."}, {"q": "How do you avoid variations during the project?", "a": "By writing exclusions and interfaces into the brief and having each candidate confirm what is included. An unwritten exclusion always becomes a variation."}, {"q": "Is a mock-up room mandatory?", "a": "Not legally, but it is the only way to fix a common reference between the brief, production and reception. Without it, every room becomes a first validation."}, {"q": "How long should the tender phase take?", "a": "It depends on the number of packages and how complete the drawings are. The question phase and the analysis often take as long as writing the brief itself."}, {"q": "How do you handle an operating hotel's constraints?", "a": "By stating delivery and installation windows per floor, authorised access and storage areas in the brief, because those constraints change installation cost and programming."}]],
"ressources/phaser-renovation-hotel": ["Phasing a hotel renovation: guestrooms, reception and dining", "Phasing a hotel renovation", "A method for splitting a hotel renovation by space — guestrooms, reception and dining — and for setting out the stages and responsibilities to confirm.", "Hotel renovations are rarely delivered in one phase. Splitting the work by space — guestrooms, reception, dining — keeps the property trading and lets you compare proposals on the same scope.", [["Why split the renovation by space","The split follows how the property operates, not how a catalogue is arranged. A guestroom wing can close while reception stays in service; the reverse is rarely possible. For each space, record downtime windows, on-site storage constraints and assembly access before you compare proposals."],["Start with the guestrooms","Guestrooms carry the largest and most repetitive volume. Group them by room type and list beds, headboards, desks and storage for each type. One approved type becomes the reference for the rest; surface differences are handled afterwards, not at the point of order."],["Plan the reception and public areas","Reception brings together a welcome zone, a working position and a circulation route. Counter dimensions, cable routes and accessibility requirements are reviewed against the plan, because a visually similar layout can serve a different use."],["Set the restaurant and dining areas","Covers, service style and storage drive the selection. Tables, seating and buffet units are reviewed together: a table selected for the dining room will not necessarily suit the terrace or breakfast service."],["Allocate responsibilities and stages","Production, transport, receiving, installation and packing removal are separate stages. Each appears in the brief with its own responsibility. That level of detail prevents two quotations from being read as the same scope."]], ["Spaces and downtime windows","Room counts by type","Circulations and assembly access","On-site storage constraints","Transport and installation responsibilities","Material and finish references"], [{"q":"Which space should a hotel renovation start with?","a":"Start with the guestrooms, because they carry the largest and most repetitive volume. One approved room type becomes the reference for the rest, which reduces adjustments later at the point of order."},{"q":"Can the hotel stay open during the renovation?","a":"Yes, provided the split follows how the property operates. A guestroom wing can close while reception stays in service. Record downtime windows, storage constraints and assembly access before comparing proposals."},{"q":"Should you build a sample room before ordering?","a":"It is advisable when several room types are involved. A sample room confirms dimensions, finishes and composition, then serves as the reference for the remaining types and for surface differences."},{"q":"When should the furniture arrive on site?","a":"Furniture should arrive when each room is ready to receive it, not earlier. Production, transport, receiving and installation are separate stages, so agree the delivery date against the works programme rather than in isolation."},{"q":"Does phasing make a renovation more expensive?","a":"It can add cost lines: on-site storage, split deliveries, protection of occupied areas and extra handling. These depend on the number of phases and site access, and belong in the itemised brief rather than a general estimate."}]],
"ressources/mobilier-chambre-hotel-niveau-gamme": ["Hotel guestroom furniture: what actually changes across service tiers", "Guestroom furniture tiers", "Carcass, edge banding, runners, fabrics, foam, beds and hardware: how to read a hotel guestroom's tier and write it into measurable specifications.", "Three-star, four-star, boutique or five-star: the gap is not mainly a matter of style, but of what survives use. Two units that look alike may carry 0.4 mm or 2 mm edging, a roller runner or a full-extension runner with soft close. The markers below describe ranges seen in hotel work and are to be confirmed against supplier documentation. Here are the points that genuinely change, and how to turn them into parameters you can inspect before comparing proposals. These markers are there to frame the right questions, not to rank a supplier.", [["Tier shows first in the carcass","An entry-level unit often uses 16 mm particleboard with 0.4 to 0.8 mm applied edging. A higher tier moves to 18 or 19 mm board with 1.5 to 2 mm ABS edging, laid flat or flush. Band thickness matters as much as the adhesive: thin edging lifts at the first suitcase knock, and the repair shows. Behind the front, the runner decides the feel — roller, ball-bearing, then full extension with soft close. Ask for the rated load per pair, the travel and the cycle count tested. The back panel, often a thin stapled sheet, deserves the same scrutiny as the face, particularly when it carries drawers. Adjustable shelves on pins, rather than fixed battens, let a cabinet be reconfigured without tools, and that flexibility belongs in the brief. Fixings count too: wood screws in a soft board, cam fittings or inserts in a dense one. Specifying by brand name alone leaves the tier open to interpretation, so describe the assembly instead."],["Fabrics, foam and removability","Abrasion resistance is read in Martindale rubs. A lightly used guestroom often holds between 20,000 and 25,000 rubs; a standard hotel room sits nearer 30,000 to 40,000; heavily used seating goes beyond 50,000. These figures are checked on the supplier's report, not on the label. Fire treatment and its classification are confirmed against the regulations of the destination country. A removable cover changes maintenance: closure type, fabric direction, washing behaviour and dimensional stability. For foam, 30 to 40 kg/m³ suits a back panel; a seat calls for a firmer density and a worked thickness, and the two should be specified separately. One spare cover per room type avoids taking a room out of service for a single stain, so state the washing regime you expect. Pile direction and shade are checked on a fitted cover, not on a sample lying flat. State whether covers are washed in-house or contracted out, because that changes the closure and the seam allowance."],["The bed: frame, headboard and support","The bed concentrates the most expensive things to correct. A basic frame butts panels together; a higher tier uses solid wood or plywood legs, corner brackets, a central rail and adjustable feet. Beech or birch slats sit in holders that limit squeaking: their number and section matter more than their finish. Headboards fix in three ways — wall rail, integrated into the frame, or suspended — and a wall rail, which limits vibration, depends on the wall build-up. The mattress support, whether slats, a base or a platform, has to be described: it drives comfort and the replacement route later on. Headboard height follows the mattress top rather than the mood board: give it as a dimension from finished floor and check it against the bedside table. A close-slatted base and a sprung base are replaced differently once the floor is trading. Ask how the headboard is accessed for reupholstery without removing the bed."],["Hardware, lighting and power points","Hardware decides how the room feels in use. A damped hinge with three-axis adjustment can be reset after settlement; a plain hinge only opens. Handles hold through bolted fixings or inserts: adhesive alone gives way. Mirrors integrate side or perimeter lighting, with colour temperature and colour rendering stated, and the ingress protection rating matters in the bathroom. On the electrical side, a guestroom needs sockets at the bedside, USB-A and USB-C ports, and a free point for a charger. These details weigh little at design stage and a great deal to add later, once the walls are closed. A cable route cut at machining stage avoids drilling on site and the making good that follows, so show it on the furniture drawing. Door buffers and drawer bumpers cut noise, which matters along a corridor at night. Bedside controls should be reachable from the pillow without stretching."],["Storage, maintenance and the specification","Storage is drawn from use: rail height, number of adjustable shelves, internal drawers, wardrobe lighting, space for a suitcase and for a safe. A luggage rack is chosen for its height and load, not its look. A minibar needs a ventilated niche and a dedicated socket. Maintenance is prepared at order stage: removable fronts, replaceable panels, interchangeable headboard covers, identified spare parts. Five-year serviceability is judged on those possibilities, not on a promise. Finally, the specification translates tier into parameters: board and edging thickness, runner load and travel, rub count, foam density, fixing method. Those are the values that get inspected on delivery. A spares schedule with references and quantities is handed over with the room file, and that document is what keeps a floor running in year four. Safe niche dimensions and usable wardrobe depth are measured on site, not taken from the architect’s plan. Photograph the inside of a finished wardrobe; it is the part guests judge daily."]], ["Board and edging thickness in millimetres","Runner load rating, travel and cycle count","Martindale rub count and fire treatment class","Foam density and removable cover specification","Headboard fixing method and wall build-up","Wardrobe, safe, minibar and power point positions"], [{"q":"How do you tell which tier a hotel guestroom furniture piece belongs to?","a":"Look at the carcass before the finish: board thickness, edge band thickness and bond, runner type. An 18 or 19 mm board with 1.5 to 2 mm ABS edging and full-extension runners sits above a 16 mm construction with thin tape."},{"q":"How should the furniture tier be written into a specification?","a":"Turn each requirement into a value that can be inspected: board and edging thickness, runner load and travel, Martindale rub count, foam density, headboard fixing. Those figures can be checked at delivery, whereas a trade label cannot."},{"q":"Which parts of guestroom furniture wear out first?","a":"Drawer fronts and their edges, runners, handles and hinges go first, followed by seat covers at contact points. Bed frames and slats come next, particularly where adjustable feet were left out."},{"q":"Can entry-level guestroom furniture be upgraded to a higher tier later?","a":"Some parts can be renewed, such as covers, cushions, runners and handles, but the carcass, board thickness and edging cannot be upgraded without rebuilding the unit. Settle those three points at order stage."},{"q":"What should you send when requesting a hotel guestroom furniture proposal?","a":"Send dimensioned room plans, the intended use of each room type, your specification parameters, proposed finishes and fabrics, plus fixing and cable-route constraints. Photographs of the existing room help confirm dimensions before anything is drawn."}]],
"ressources/restaurant-places-mobilier": ["How many covers to plan for before ordering restaurant furniture", "Calculating restaurant covers", "Turning restaurant floor area into a cover count: deductions, table formats, aisle widths, service style and a zone-by-zone seating table.", "The cover count is settled before the tables are chosen, not after. A room sized by eye and then furnished often ends up with aisles that are too tight or rows that cannot be sold, and a layout is hard to correct once the furniture is ordered. Area, deductions, table formats and circulation turn into a simple schedule that every proposal can follow and challenge. The aim is not to fill the room, but to reach a cover count that holds up in service. Here is how to move from floor area to a number of covers, and which margins to check on site before the order is fixed.", [["Start from usable seating area, not total floor area","A 180 m² dining room does not offer 180 m² of seats. Deduct the bar, the buffet, service stations, exits and circulation first, then apply 1.2 to 1.8 m² per cover for table service, or 0.9 to 1.2 m² for quick service and cafés. These figures are planning references, not a delivery commitment: room shape, columns, banquettes and the chosen furniture all change the result. For example, 180 m² less 25 m² of bar, 18 m² of buffet and 20 per cent circulation leaves about 110 m² of seating, or 60 to 90 covers depending on the comfort level sought. Keep the density consistent with the service you advertise, and calculate a terrace separately."],["Choose table formats that can be combined","A 700 to 800 mm square table seats two; two of them pushed together make a four, provided the bases do not clash and the tops line up. A 1200 to 1400 mm by 800 mm rectangular table does the same job without handling. A round table of 1200 to 1400 mm in diameter seats four to six and softens the corners of a room. A long table suits groups and narrow rooms. Check the height under the top, usually 720 to 750 mm, and the footprint of the bases, which decides real legroom. Mixing formats only helps if heights, edges and finishes stay compatible, and if a split pair does not block an aisle."],["Set the aisles and the clearances","Three widths structure the room: 1200 mm for the main aisle, 900 mm for the service aisle, and 900 mm between the table edge and the wall so a chair can be pulled out. Between two occupied tables, allow 900 to 1000 mm from chair back to chair back; below that, guests rise sideways and service slows down. These are working minimums: check them on a scaled plan with the actual furniture, and against the accessibility and fire safety rules that apply to the premises. Add fixed obstacles — columns, radiators, doors — before counting. A 1200 mm aisle on paper can become 600 mm once chairs are pulled out."],["Service style changes the arithmetic","Plated service holds a table for longer than a buffet or a counter does. The cover count therefore cannot be read from floor area alone: it depends on how long guests stay and how many services you run. Estimate turnover per service, then check that the kitchen and bar can keep up; adding covers without extra kitchen throughput simply moves the wait into the dining room. Bar seating counts differently: a stool takes less floor than a table but stays occupied for longer. An all-day lobby bar is not sized like a dinner room. Count standing places and stools as separate categories, not as tables, and note that a counter turns faster at lunch than at dinner."],["Terrace, breakfast and the seating table","On a terrace and at breakfast, furniture has to move, stack and withstand exposure. Allow a store or a corner for tables that are not in use, and check the weight of each piece if staff handle it alone. Anticipate wind, sun and rain, which drive the choice of materials and the storage arrangement. At breakfast, density can be higher and occupancy shorter. Then set out a table by zone: gross area, deductions, table formats, resulting covers. That table becomes the reference document for comparing proposals; the five points in the checklist still need confirming on site. Loose furniture also makes cleaning and evening re-sets easier."]], ["Zone table: gross area, deductions, formats, covers obtained","Target covers per service and expected turnover, bar and terrace included","Aisle widths measured on site, not only on the plan","Table formats and two-plus-two assembly","Bar, buffet and service stations positioned before counting","Terrace: storage, stacking, exposure, weight of pieces"], [{"q":"How much floor area should I allow per cover?","a":"Allow 1.2 to 1.8 m² per cover for table service and 0.9 to 1.2 m² for quick service. Apply these densities to usable seating area, after deducting the bar, buffet and circulation, and check them on a scaled plan."},{"q":"What is the minimum aisle width in a restaurant?","a":"Allow 1200 mm for the main aisle and 900 mm for the service aisle. Between two occupied tables, keep 900 to 1000 mm from chair back to chair back, then measure again with chairs pulled out."},{"q":"Are round tables better than square tables?","a":"Neither is better in every room. A 700 to 800 mm square table seats two and pairs into a four; a round table of 1200 to 1400 mm seats four to six and softens corners. Mix them only if heights and finishes match."},{"q":"Should terrace seats be included in the cover count?","a":"Yes, but as a separate figure. A terrace has its own area, furniture and storage needs, because wind, rain and sun require pieces that move and stack. Add the two zones only after checking each layout."},{"q":"When should the furniture specification be fixed?","a":"After the zone table is complete, not before. Usable area, deductions, table formats and aisle widths need to be settled and checked on site, because a layout is hard to correct once the furniture is ordered."}]],
"ressources/amenagement-lobby-hotel-zones-circulation": ["Hotel Lobby Furniture: Zones, Circulation and Selection", "Lobby furniture planning", "Five zones, measured clear widths and fabrics chosen by use: how to furnish a hotel lobby without over-specifying the quiet corners or under-specifying the bar.", "A lobby is not a room furnished in one gesture. It is a sequence of moments a guest crosses in seconds, suitcase in hand. Reception, waiting, work, bar and the circulation spine share neither the same clear widths, nor the same fabrics, nor the same service life. The markers below are ranges seen in hotel work and are to be confirmed on plan and against supplier documentation. They are there to frame the right questions before comparing proposals, not to rank a supplier.", [["Read the lobby as five zones, not one room","A lobby is read on foot, not from a plan. From the entrance to the desk, then from the desk to the lifts, a guest passes through at least four atmospheres in a few seconds: he puts down a case, looks for a reference point, waits, sometimes sits, orders a coffee, leaves. Each moment calls for different furniture and, above all, a different clear width. Splitting the lobby into five zones — arrival, waiting, work and social, bar, circulation spine — stops the room being treated as one batch of seating.\n\nThe method needs three surveys. First the spine: the most direct line between entrance, reception and lifts, kept at 1.20 m clear minimum, widening to 1.50 m at crossings and in front of the queue. Then the contact points: hands on armrests, cases against benches, trays set on low tables. Finally the dimensions of each group, taken between furniture rather than between walls, because a chair pushed back 20 cm eats a passage.\n\nThat split has a direct consequence for the tender pack. A lobby specified with one fabric and one foam density will be over-specified in the quiet corners and under-specified around the bar. Separating the zones lets each carry its own rub count, its own cover type and its own replacement logic, without paying twice for the same performance."],["Arrival and reception: the first three metres","The arrival zone carries the hardest-working furniture in the lobby: reception desk, counter return, low console, luggage bench, high stools for standing waits, umbrella stand. The worktop usually sits between 1,050 and 1,150 mm, with an accessible position at 750–800 mm, 700 mm clear knee width and 450 mm knee depth. In front of the desk, allow 1,200 mm clear for a guest with a case and a companion; behind it, 900 mm for the chair and staff movement. A luggage bench sits at 450–500 mm seat height, 400–450 mm deep, with a top protected by an applied edge.\n\nThe desk front takes trolleys, cases and belt buckles. A high-pressure laminate face or a veneer with 2 mm ABS edging can be repaired locally; a lacquer far less so. Treat the plinth as a wear part: removable kick plate, through-bolted fixings, a colour close to the floor to hide scuffs. High stools in the waiting position need vinyl at 50,000 Martindale minimum, a seat with no seam across the front, and replaceable glides.\n\nOne detail is often missed: the queue. Without a floor line or a drop-off bench, guests stop at the desk and block the spine. A waiting seat 1,500 mm from the desk, or a 400 mm deep console for bags, often clears it. Add a socket near the bench and lighting that makes the desk readable without glare in the eyes of someone seated."],["Waiting: set the spacing before choosing the chair","In the waiting zone, comfort comes down to simple dimensions. A lobby armchair sits at 420–450 mm, is 480–520 mm deep and allows 600–700 mm width per place. Between two chairs, a 400–450 mm diameter side table needs 400–450 mm clear on each side. Two chairs facing each other sit 800–900 mm apart so knees do not touch; behind the backs, allow 1,200 mm where the circulation spine runs. Check these on a 1:20 plan, not on a mood image.\n\nWaiting upholstery works through prolonged contact rather than hard abrasion: 40,000 to 50,000 Martindale for a lobby seat, 25,000 to 30,000 for a back. Seat foam at 35–40 kg/m³ holds its shape; below that the seat compacts and the front edge collapses. A removable cover with a concealed closure and the pile running the right way lets a cushion be turned or replaced without taking the group out of service. A pale velvet soils at the back and armrests: choose a mid-tone or a tight weave.\n\nThe real cost of lobby seating is not read at order stage but at the first replacement. Three decisions reduce it: cushions independent of the back, covers available separately, a demountable frame. In use, cushions turn, covers wash, a frame is repaired without scrapping the seat. A fully upholstered and glued seat, by contrast, forces the whole chair out for one stain or one local collapse."],["Work and social: a table that takes a laptop","The work-and-social zone now hosts mixed uses: a guest answering messages, two colleagues preparing a meeting, a traveller waiting for a taxi. A bar-height table does not work for seated laptop use; 740–750 mm height, 600 mm usable depth per person and 700 mm seat width remain the baseline. For a shared table, allow 600 mm width per place and 1,200 mm between back-to-back rows once chairs are pushed back. A fixed banquette saves space but requires the seat depth (450–480 mm) and the table clearance (250–300 mm) to be calculated.\n\nPower points decide the real use. Two sockets per table, one of them USB-C, with a cable route cut at machining stage rather than drilled on site, remove visible extension leads. The top should stay matt under lighting: a glossy laminate throws the screen back. On acoustics, a stone-and-glass lobby sends conversation towards reception; absorbent panels under table tops or inside seats, plus heavy textiles, lower the level without dividing the room.\n\nIn the social zone, table edges, armrests and backs wear first. A 2 mm edge, a top replaceable independently of the base and armrests supplied as separate parts change the maintenance bill over time. Avoid tops bonded permanently to the base: one deep scratch then means removing the whole table."],["Bar and circulation spine: what fails first","Around the bar, dimensions start from the counter. For a counter at 1,050–1,100 mm, a stool sets at 750–780 mm seat height, with a footrest around 450 mm. Allow 250–300 mm between stools, a 300–350 mm counter overhang for the knees, and 1,200 mm clear behind occupied stools — more where a stool pulls back. Without that overhang, knees meet the face of the counter, the guest sits at an angle and the seat edge wears.\n\nThe main spine stays at 1,200 mm, widening to 1,500 mm at crossings, in front of the lifts and at the restaurant entrance. Low furniture — consoles, planters, display stands — belongs outside that spine, at least 300 mm from door leaves. Floor changes need a flat profile: a poorly set threshold bar trips guests and tears the glides off stools.\n\nThe bar stool is the most-replaced item in a lobby: seat edge, seams, glides, footrest. Technical vinyl at 100,000 Martindale, a moulded seat with no seam across the front and a metal frame with screwed glides last better than furnishing fabric. Finally, separate quantities by zone and wear parts in the tender pack: seats, covers, tops, glides. That separation is what allows one batch of stools to be replaced without touching the waiting chairs, and keeps a lobby coherent after several years of trading."]], ["Clear width of the main spine and crossings","Reception worktop and accessible counter heights","Seat spacing and clearance behind armchairs","Martindale rub count by zone and removable covers","Foam density and cushion replaceability","Sockets, cable routes and foot protection"], [{"q":"How much clear width does a hotel lobby need for circulation?","a":"Allow 1.20 m clear on the main spine between entrance, reception and lifts, and 1.50 m at crossings, in front of the queue and at the restaurant entrance. Measure between furniture rather than between walls, and check it with the chairs pushed back."},{"q":"How do you choose an armchair for a hotel lobby waiting area?","a":"Start with dimensions: 420–450 mm seat height, 480–520 mm depth, 600–700 mm per place, and 800–900 mm between chairs facing each other. Then confirm foam at 35–40 kg/m³ in the seat, a removable cover and a fabric of 40,000 to 50,000 Martindale."},{"q":"Which fabric holds up best in a busy hotel lobby?","a":"Technical vinyl or coated fabric at 100,000 Martindale for bar stools, and 40,000 to 50,000 for waiting seats. Avoid pale shades at contact points, and ask for the supplier test report rather than relying on the label."},{"q":"How should circulation be organised between reception and the bar?","a":"Keep a direct 1.20 m spine from entrance to desk to lifts, widening to 1.50 m at crossings. Keep low furniture out of that spine and at least 300 mm from door leaves, and set the queue 1.50 m back from the desk."},{"q":"Which spare parts should be planned for a hotel lobby?","a":"Seat covers and cushions, glides and feet, table tops, desk kick plates and footrests. Ask for them as separate lines in the tender pack: that is what allows one wear part to be replaced without taking a whole seating group out of service."}]],
"ressources/mobilier-hall-hotel-variations-devis": ["Hotel lobby furniture: what moves the quote", "Understanding the lobby quote", "Furniture families, customization level, invisible specifications, packaging and services: what explains the gap between two hotel lobby furniture quotes.", "Two quotes prepared for lobbies of comparable size can diverge widely, and the gap is almost never explained by the manufacturer’s margin alone. It comes from what each quote understands by “lobby furniture”: which families are included, at what customization level, with which internal specifications and which services. This guide breaks those factors down so the proposals can be brought back to a comparable basis.", [["Why do two quotes for the same lobby diverge?","The first cause is scope. A quote covering seating, tables, the reception desk and accessories is not comparable to one limited to seating: the rest reappears later as add-on costs. The second cause is the customization level; an adjusted catalogue piece is not priced like an original drawing with a dedicated prototype and sample. The third gathers the invisible specifications — frame, cushioning, testing — that the mood image never shows. The fourth is logistics: packaging, transport and site reception can stay outside the displayed amount."],["Which furniture families should the quote include?","A complete quote names each family on its own line: waiting and lounge seating, coffee and side tables, the reception desk, and accessories such as a luggage bench, space divider, planters or a coat stand. Each family carries its quantity and its control document: a workshop drawing for the reception desk, a material sheet for the seating, a packing scheme for the whole set. A quote that does not cite the document usually has not budgeted what it covers. Preparing the lobby with the same method as the hotel furniture schedule prevents omissions that turn into supplements."],["Customization: catalogue, adapted or bespoke?","Three levels coexist. Catalogue adjustment keeps the existing design and changes only the fabric, lacquer or wood tone; tooling is shared across projects. Adaptation changes dimensions, adds a socket or reinforces a structure; extra preparation is added. Bespoke starts from your drawings: a prototype, a dedicated sample and committee validation before the series. The common mistake is to ask for a “distinctive style” with a catalogue budget; when the committee validates an original drawing at the third round, the prototype rework returns as a separate line if the quote excluded it."],["Which invisible specifications weigh on the price?","Three layers escape the presentation image. Structure first: frame sections, material and reinforcements at the support points; intensive-use seating is designed for non-domestic usage tests. Comfort next: foam density and height, the suspension system and the resilience of the filling; high-resilience foam keeps its shape after thousands of cycles and its raw material shows in the quote. Compliance finally: the fire behaviour of the fabrics and the lot documents that allow each batch to be traced. To check this layer, require a technical sheet that cites the test, a physical sample approved against that document, and the right to inspect during production."],["How to bring two quotes to a common basis?","Before placing two amounts side by side, align five points: family scope, customization level, seating specification, packing standard and delivery term. Each gap becomes a precise question: are the prototype and the sample included or billed separately, is export packing inside the price, which on-site works stay separate. The detailed method for comparing quotations on a common basis provides the framework; applied to the lobby, it turns a price gap into a list of different scopes."],["Which costs appear after the signature?","Three sources recur. Packaging and transport: a figure on FOB terms covers neither the container, nor the insurance, nor the delivery to site. Installation: a lobby is fitted in a hotel in operation or in an opening on a tight schedule, with service-lift and night-work constraints. Revision rounds: every change after the approved sample reopens production, and quotes limit the number of free rounds. Ask for that number to be written in the quote, not in a later email."]], ["Furniture scope line by line","Customization level per family","Technical sheet with cited test for seating","Sample approved against document","Packing standard and delivery term","Number of revision rounds included"], [{"question":"Which documents should be requested before a first price?","answer":"The dimensioned plan of the lobby, the zoning, and for each zone the expected families with their customization level. These elements are enough to frame a comparable scope; a price requested without a written scope produces amounts that are hard to compare."},{"question":"Why does a lobby seat cost more than a domestic one?","answer":"Intensive use multiplies seating cycles and cleaning operations. The frame, the foam density and the fabrics are then specified for that usage and verified through tests, which weighs on the price but protects the replacement budget."},{"question":"Is export packing part of the price?","answer":"It depends on the agreed sales term. Ask for the packing standard, the internal protection and the palletising to appear as a line of the quote, so you know whether the displayed amount covers protection up to site reception."},{"question":"How many revision rounds should be planned?","answer":"Plan tone or material adjustments before the sample approval; after approval, every design change reopens production. Have the number of revision rounds included in the quote written down, together with the point at which a rework becomes billable."},{"question":"Should the lobby be grouped with the other common areas?","answer":"Grouping widens the discussion and aligns finishes, provided the quote stays split by space. You keep the option to approve, postpone or lighten each space without renegotiating the whole."}]],
"ressources/tete-de-lit-hotel-prises-lecture-protection": ["Hotel headboard wall: power outlets, reading lights and wall protection", "Specify the headboard wall", "Mounting heights for outlets and reading lights, bedside control, wall-protection finishes and plan-level coordination: the decisions that make a hotel headboard wall work from the first night of operation.", "The headboard wall concentrates, on a few square metres, most of the rework reported after a guest room opens: an outlet nobody can reach, a reading lamp aimed at the reader's face, a finish marked by rolled headboards. The cause is almost never the furniture itself; it is late coordination between the electrical plan, the furniture shop drawings and the choice of finishes. This article sets out the decisions to settle on plan, in which order, and which documents to freeze before ordering.", [["Why is the headboard wall decided before the bed is chosen?","In a hotel guest room the headboard wall is not decoration; it is an interface. On one side sit joinery and upholstery, on the other electrical points, lighting control and finishes that must survive daily use. Until the bed width, the nightstand positions and the headboard overhang are fixed, outlet and lamp positions remain hanging. If the electrician works ahead of the shop drawings, points end up behind solid panels or, worse, inside the volume a nightstand hides. The correct order is therefore: room layout frozen, wall shop drawings approved, then electrical points transferred onto the wall elevation. That sequence removes most rework and applies to renovation as much as to new build."],["At what height should outlets and reading lights be mounted?","Usual heights are settled on plan, together with the electrician and the architect, from three references: finished floor level, the top of the mattress or made-up bed, and the top of the nightstand. An outlet set too low becomes unreachable once the headboard is installed; a lamp set too high lights the reader's face instead of the page. The reliable method is to draw the wall at true scale, trace a seated reader on it, and place every point relative to that silhouette rather than to the bare wall. Each project then fixes its own dimensions; they are frozen on the execution drawing and checked on the mock-up before series production."],["Reading lights: what aiming and what control?","A hotel reading lamp is judged on three things: beam aim, control, and independence of each side. The beam must light the page without crossing the bed axis; it is the lamp body orientation, more than its output, that decides comfort. Control must be reachable without sitting up, from the seated position, and independent of the room's master switch. Finally, each side of the bed is switched separately: two guests do not read at the same time. On plan these requirements translate into one technical sheet per luminaire and one control point per side, transferred onto the wall elevation together with the furniture shop drawings."],["Wall protection: what should be specified above and around the bed?","The most stressed band of the wall is the one no render shows: the strip where heads rest, where luggage leans during housekeeping, where the bed runner rubs at every turn-down. Finishes there are chosen to be washable and repairable: lacquered timber panels or laminates for the frame, coated technical fabrics for soft surfaces, fold-down protection behind the headboard where connecting rooms are planned. The specification separates the contact zone, which receives the toughest finish, from the upper bands, which can be more decorative. These zones are defined line by line on the elevation, and every finish is validated by an approved sample against a document."],["How is the wall coordinated with the furniture on plan?","Coordination happens on a single view: the headboard wall elevation, on which the headboard, nightstands, electrical points and lamps are overlaid. Three checks are systematic. First, does each outlet axis fall outside the volume hidden by the headboard and outside the volume occupied by the nightstand? Second, does the nightstand top edge leave the outlet reachable without moving furniture? Third, have the joinery installation tolerances (a few millimetres per panel) absorbed the allowance needed for cable routes? This one view, signed by both the furniture and electrical packages before ordering, replaces weeks of email loops during installation."],["Which documents should be frozen before ordering?","Four documents are enough to secure a headboard wall. The dimensioned architectural plan of the room, which provides the height references. The coordinated electrical plan, where every point is numbered and transferred onto the wall elevation. The furniture shop drawings with dimensioned sections, which show what the joinery hides and what it leaves accessible. And the sign-off report of the mock-up, which validates a sample room in the workshop or on site before series production. As a space reference, a hotel project in Zanzibar (Tanzania) documents guest rooms whose headboard walls combine timber panels, wall lamps and floating nightstands; those images serve to discuss compositions, they do not describe a delivery in any specific country and involve no brand."]], ["Wall layout dimensioned on the architectural plan","Electrical points: outlets, USB, switches coordinated","Reading lights: model, aiming, control per side","Protection finishes by contact zone and upper band","Furniture alignment: headboard and nightstands on one elevation","Mock-up and signed validation report before series"], [{"question":"Should outlets be integrated into the furniture or the wall?","answer":"Both work and the choice is made on the shared elevation. A wall outlet stays reachable if it falls outside the headboard and nightstand volumes; an outlet integrated into the nightstand shortens cables but makes the furniture depend on the electrical package. The plan signed by both packages settles it."},{"question":"What height should reading lights be mounted at?","answer":"The height is fixed on the true-scale wall elevation by tracing a seated reader: the beam should reach the page without lighting the face. Final dimensions are frozen on the execution drawing and checked on the mock-up."},{"question":"Is a bedside lighting control necessary?","answer":"Yes, a control reachable from the seated position, independent of the room master switch, and separate for each side of the bed. It is the point most often reported by housekeeping teams after opening."},{"question":"Which finish should be chosen for wall protection?","answer":"The direct contact zone receives the toughest finish: lacquered timber, laminate or washable coated technical fabric. Upper bands, less stressed, can be more decorative. Every finish is validated by an approved sample against a document."},{"question":"How can post-installation rework be avoided?","answer":"By freezing four documents before ordering: the dimensioned plan, the coordinated electrical plan, the furniture shop drawings and the mock-up sign-off report. Coordination happens on one elevation signed by both packages, not on email exchanges during installation."}]],
};

